      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. so for the /s/ sound, it's basically just the air/friction without the vocal cords doing anything? is there a way to physically feel the difference if i put my hand on my throat while saying them?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, the main difference is that the /s/ sound has air friction without beat or vibration of the true vocal cords. When you put your hand on your throat while saying /s/, you might feel a slight vibration or \"buzzing\" sound that's relatively muffled and subtle. This buzzing is associated with the vibration of your vocal cords, even if the sound itself is unvoiced.\n\nIn contrast, the /z/ sound typically has more pronounced vibration, and you may feel a more noticeable buzz or \"friction hum\" in your throat when you put your hand there. This increased vibration can give the impression that the sound is \"voiced\" or more resonant, even though it's still not fully a voiced sound.",
