    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In phonetics, we often refer to the quality of a consonantal sound using terms like \"friction\" (general), \"aspirated\" (weakly voiced or unclear), \"ejective\" (intensely explosive), \"recurrent\" (diphthong-like quality), and \"thalatal\" ( closely related to alveolar fricatives).\n\nFor /z/ and /s/, in English phonetics and transcriptions like the International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA), we might describe the sounds as having a \"thin, sharp\" or \"tense, labiodental suffix\" being substituted with /s/ which implies a hard-edge glottal coda, but it generally use a flow like tongue and friction.",
